":86138646189234,"name":"Soul y R&B","book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","start":1960,"end":1968,"desc":"El 1 de enero de 1966 el Top 3 estadounidense lo formaban «The Sound of Silence», «We Can Work It Out» y el «I Got You (I Feel Good)» de James Brown: discos futuristas los tres. A finales de mes Otis Redding se acercaba al Top 10 británico con su versión de «My Girl» de los Temptations.","color":"#facc15","family":"Soul, Motown, R&B y ska","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#soul-rnb"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":253563926130588,"artist":"Charlie Rich","artist_id":42662354908309,"title":"Mohair Sam","year":1965,"ess":0,"yt":"R4SvJv8TGE0","kind":null,"book":{"id":1,"title":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/"},"scene":"Soul y R&B","scene_id":86138646189234,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#soul-rnb","chapters":["Julio: Land of 1000 Dances: Tamla, el soul y la Marcha contra el Miedo"],"pages":[[1,186]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","page":186,"text":"…‘Agent 00 Soul’ – Edwin Starr 35. ‘Money’ – Barrett Strong 36. ‘Some Other Guy’ – Richie Barrett 37. ‘It Wasn’t Me’ – Chuck Berry 38. ‘Mohair Sam’ – Charlie Rich 39. ‘Let Him Run Wild’ – The Beach Boys 40. ‘Do You Believe in Magic’ – The Lovin’ Spoonful Thirty-four out of the forty-two songs listed are black…"},{"book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","page":419,"text":"…you dig?’ Dylan answers: ‘Rimbaud, I guess; W. C. Fields; The family, you know, the trapeze family in the circus; Smokey Robinson; Allen Ginsberg; Charlie Rich – he’s a good poet.’ Read more at www.rollingstone.com/music/news/bob-dylan-givespress-conference-in-san-francisco-19671214 and…"}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"1974 CMA Entertainer of the Year","body":"Charlie Rich was honored with the Entertainer of the Year award at the 1974 Country Music Association (CMA) Awards, which represents the highest accolade given by the CMA. \"The following year, at the 1974 CMAs, he was named Entertainer of the Year, the CMA's highest honor.\"","year":1974,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Country Roads and Summer Nights Edition Part 1","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"1974 CMA Entertainer of the Year Win","body":"Charlie Rich won the CMA Entertainer of the Year award in 1974, reaping rewards from his successful 1973 work. \"As the 1974 winner, he was reaping the rewards of his work that he had done in 1973.\"","year":1974,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 2 - Countryish Music of the 1970s (Encore)","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"1975 CMA Award Presentation Incident","body":"During the 1975 CMAs, Charlie Rich was scheduled to present the New Entertainer of the Year award. Upon opening the envelope and seeing John Denver's name, Rich controversially used a lighter to set the slip of paper on fire before announcing Denver as the winner. This bizarre act was later attributed to Rich being intoxicated and under the influence of pain medication, with him reportedly believing it was humorous. However, to the CMA audience, it was widely perceived as a protest against the increasing \"popification\" of country music, a sentiment that had been recently fueled by debates involving Olivia Newton-John. \"rich, open the envelope, and when he saw John Denver's name there, took out a lighter and lit the slip of paper on fire.\"","year":1975,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Country Roads and Summer Nights Edition Part 1","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"1975 CMA Awards Incident - Card Burning","body":"As the reigning CMA entertainer of the year, Charlie Rich staggered onto the stage at Nashville's Grand Ole Opry in 1975, fueled by gin and tonics, and announced John Denver as the winner. He then pulled out a lighter and set the card with Denver's name on it on fire, an act widely interpreted as a statement that John Denver was not welcome in country music. Rich later claimed it was an accident caused by pain killers. \"Rich reaches into his pocket, takes out a lighter and sets the card with Denver's name on it on fire.\"","year":1975,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 2 - Countryish Music of the 1970s (Encore)","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"CMA Banning After Incident","body":"As a direct consequence of his controversial paper-burning incident during the 1975 CMA Awards, Charlie Rich was subsequently banned by the CMAs for an unspecified period of years. \"After the show, the CMAs banned Charlie Rich for a period of years.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Country Roads and Summer Nights Edition Part 1","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Crossover Success Irony","body":"Despite his public act of defiance at the 1975 CMAs, which was interpreted as a protest against the \"popification\" of country music, Charlie Rich himself had previously achieved significant crossover success, having topped the pop charts with his own music. \"This was, well, a bit rich coming from Charlie Rich, because after all, he too had crossed over and topped the pop charts.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Country Roads and Summer Nights Edition Part 1","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Drinking Habits","body":"It was well-known that Charlie Rich frequently drank heavily, which was cited as a factor in his behavior at the 1975 CMA Awards. \"It was no secret then or now that Charlie Rich drank and he drank a lot.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 2 - Countryish Music of the 1970s (Encore)","url":null}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Unreleased Sun Recordings","body":"Charlie Rich had numerous unreleased songs stored in Sun Records' vaults. Shelby Singleton, upon acquiring Sun, ensured that pretty much every scrap of material from these vaults, including Rich's, saw release. \"In their four-year Rocket Billy Hayday, Sam Phillips and Jack Clements had recorded literally thousands of unreleased songs by Jerry Lee Lewis, Johnny Cash, Roy Orbison, Karl Perkins, Conway Twitty, Charlie Rich, Billy Lee Riley, and more.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 66: \"Great Balls of Fire\" by Jerry Lee Lewis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CVp5e4EWCqI"}]}],"awards":[],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":150142947214804,"type":"song","name":"Lonely Weekends","work_year":1960,"list_id":132782249584307,"list":"Billboard Year-End 1960","src":"singles","rank":99,"year":1960,"note":"","yt":"-1YD_Ejb450","scaruffi":null},{"id":150142947214804,"type":"song","name":"Lonely Weekends","work_year":1960,"list_id":132335824845498,"list":"Billboard Year-End Hot 100","src":"charts","rank":99,"year":1960,"note":null,"yt":"-1YD_Ejb450","scaruffi":null},{"id":224474139132280,"type":"album","name":"The Many New Sides of Charlie Rich","list_id":63534269289112,"list":"RYM Top — R&B — Country Soul","src":"musthear","rank":63,"year":1965,"note":null,"yt":"0U-Tnk5paMk","scaruffi":null},{"id":199076755892477,"type":"album","name":"The Best Years","list_id":63534269289112,"list":"RYM Top — R&B — Country Soul","src":"musthear","rank":111,"year":1966,"note":null,"yt":"GSFsAtVglH0","scaruffi":null},{"id":172358690200188,"type":"album","name":"Set Me Free","list_id":222913818870117,"list":"RYM Top — Country — Nashville Sound","src":"musthear","rank":84,"year":1968,"note":null,"yt":"UV66mwCxelc","scaruffi":null},{"id":172358690200188,"type":"album","name":"Set Me Free","list_id":63534269289112,"list":"RYM Top — R&B — Country Soul","src":"musthear","rank":57,"